WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.640
This video explores the possibility of integrating your own applications into AvNav.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.880
AvNav already includes several pre-installed apps.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:24.040
The first part of the video will discuss the limitations and advantages of user apps.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:28.400
Wouldn't it be great to be able to integrate the operation of your autopilot,

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.360
your own apps for displaying data, or, for example, a logbook directly into the

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.200
AvNav interface? This is possible, but with one important

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.280
limitation: the apps must be browser-based. This means

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.360
their functions cannot run in a separate interface; instead, they are

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:54.320
launched via a URL in a browser. Let's take a look at this and move on to

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:01.360
the User Apps section. There you'll see apps that AvNav installs by default,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.800
such as the SignalK server and the history function. Other standard user apps

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.720
are located on different topic-specific pages: the update app on the server side and

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:19.240
the Mapproxy app in the map section. The button bar displays

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.760
user-installed apps such as a Node-RED dashboard,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.960
the KIP application for displaying data, and much more, as well as a

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:35.320
browser-based logbook still under development called Captain Log. How do you configure these apps so

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:43.240
they can be accessed within AvNav? Go to the "JS, CSS, User Apps" section .

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.520
The entries for user apps appear

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:53.440
in the left column . You can create new user apps using the "Add" button.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.480
However, be careful: each new app requires an identifying icon. This icon must

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:05.720
first be uploaded to AvNav or selected from the list of installed icons.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:10.320
You can do this using the "Images" button. The right column provides an

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.920
overview of the available icons for apps and buttons.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:22.360
Where do you get these icons, which should be approximately 50px x 50px?

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.960
You can easily find such images at Google Material Symbols (https://fonts.google.com/icons) or

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.720
free icon databases like lucide.dev or pixabay.com.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.320
SVG or PNG format works best;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.280
otherwise, you'll get a warning during the upload if something is wrong.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.680
Now upload your icon to AvNav using the Image button. It should then

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:54.280
be immediately visible in the Images column on the right. Next, create a new User App using Add.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.720
If the User App runs outside of AvNav, which

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:05.200
is usually the case for apps you add yourself, uncheck the "internal" box.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.320
Now enter the URL for your app, for example, the URL for KIP.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.400
If you want the User App to be accessible from anywhere on your network, the easiest way is

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:23.920
to add $HOST before the port number. Of course, you can also use your server's direct IP address.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.320
Under Icon, select the previously uploaded image for the button.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:35.480
Use short text to give the button you want to use to launch the app within AvNav

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:40.440
a descriptive name. Longtext refers to the text below the button

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:47.840
within the menu. This text can be longer and therefore more informative.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:54.920
A significant new feature, introduced with AvNav version 2026, is quite subtle: the "page"

00:03:54.920 --> 00:04:02.040
function. It allows you to assign the user app button to other AvNav pages.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.640
For example, KIP as a dashboard would be well-suited to the dashboard page,

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.360
while a logbook might belong directly on the navigation page. Ultimately,

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:18.520
everyone can configure this according to their own preferences. With "new Window," you can choose

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:23.600
to have the app open in a separate browser window or tab.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.840
That's it! Have fun setting up your user apps.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.120
I personally find it a great advantage to

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.040
be able to control the user interface of my open-source autopilot, Pypilot, directly from within AvNav.

